- Tag each food page with "Food" and the food name (e.g. "Salmon", "Lentils") so it appears in index and recipe lists.
- Tag with **intrinsic food compounds** that have (or will immediately receive) a standard or extended nutrition-table row. Use `<FoodSubstancesFromTable details={frontMatter} />` so ontology cards appear only when a matching table row exists. Do not leave a substance as a card without a table row. Use `<FoodSubstances details={frontMatter} />` only for pages not yet on the nutrition layer.
- **Do not tag with downstream metabolites** (e.g. SCFAs, butyrate, propionate, acetate, serotonin, GABA from precursors). These are mechanism truth—they may be described in prose as outcomes of the food but must not appear as food substances or BRS tags for that food.
- In the front matter, list_image: /img/icons/ingredients.svg
- The title of the page should be a tag that exactly matches the title.
- Do not tag food pages with Biological Targets.
- For the BRS matrix, tag food pages only with substance names that the food actually contains (and that appear in the nutrition table); substance pages are tagged with foods.

## Three truth-layer boundaries

This is the separate **Intrinsic / Mechanism / Strategy** content-boundary model. It determines where a claim belongs. It does not determine whether an entity has passed Overview → table → Substances admission.

- **Intrinsic food truth:** Only compounds present in the food may appear in Key Substances, nutrition table, substances list, and tags.
- **Mechanism truth:** Downstream metabolites and effects (e.g. SCFAs from fibre fermentation) may appear only in Mechanisms, BRS prose, or Overview as outcomes—never as food substances or tags.
- **Strategy truth:** Pairing and complementarity advice belong only in the EAA profile (Protein pairing strategy) or Preparation Notes/Recipes, not in Overview as substance-like content.
- **Validation:** Fail or flag if a required EAA section is missing or if downstream metabolites are proposed as food substances. Run `npm run nutrition:validate` to check. After front matter updates, run `npm run nutrition:repair` to enforce corrections at scale (or `npm run nutrition:pipeline` to apply then repair). `nutrition:pipeline` does not fetch or enrich.

## Required EAA section

- Include an **Essential Amino Acid Profile** section **only when**:
  - **Protein >= 5 g per 100 g**, OR
  - The food is **commonly used as a meaningful protein source** (e.g. grains/legumes/nuts/seeds/animal foods in typical dietary context).

- Foods with **negligible protein** (e.g. oils, fruits, low-protein vegetables) **must not** include an EAA section.

### Boundary examples (explicit)
Include EAA:
| Food | Protein (per 100g) | Reason |
|---|---:|---|
| Cod | ~18 g | Primary protein source |
| Beef | ~26 g | High-quality complete protein |
| Walnuts | ~15 g | Contributes meaningfully + limiting AAs |
| Lentils | ~9 g | Plant protein source |

Conditional / contextual:
| Food | Protein | Rule |
|---|---:|---|
| Quinoa | ~4.4 g | Include (used as protein source) |
| Oats | ~13 g | Include |
| Seeds | variable | Include if used as meaningful protein contributor |

Exclude EAA:
| Food | Protein | Reason |
|---|---:|---|
| Extra virgin olive oil | 0 g | No protein -> no amino-acid relevance |
| Blueberries | ~0.7 g | Negligible |
| Spinach | ~2.9 g | Not a protein source |
| Honey | 0 g | No protein |

Implementation notes:
- Use `protein_g` from the food page `nutrition_per_100g` as the numeric gate.
- Pairing advice belongs in the EAA section, not in Overview.

## Three sources of truth

Each food page has three layers that must stay aligned. These are the canonical **Three Sources of Truth** (`system/food-page-model.md`). Do not rename them as “standard compositional / extended analytical / biological-ontology.”

1. **Overview** – Explain what is distinctive about this food without repeating generic nutrient biology. Recipe usefulness is sufficient; do not manufacture biological importance. Culinary-support foods may be described as a meal base, binder, or culinary-support ingredient. Headline identity compounds must resolve to a supported table row or be flagged for verification. Use inline numeric citations [1], [2]. Keep the prose editorial.
2. **Database nutrition table** – Quantitative composition, primarily USDA / a named database, plus curated supplementary rows for compounds USDA does not capture. Use `<NutritionTable details={frontMatter} />`. Headings: **Core nutrients**, **Key vitamins and minerals**, **Bioactive compounds**. Do not invent values from ontology, Overview prose, or a related food. If USDA’s default abbreviated panel omits a recognised nutrient, use a richer USDA record or a curated qualitative supplementary row (`Present — quantity not established`) with a food-specific source.
3. **Substances list** – Structural ontology list via `<FoodSubstancesFromTable details={frontMatter} />`. Every tagged substance card must have a corresponding supported table row. Not every table row requires a card. Cards are not a substitute for table inclusion and are not BRS mappings.

**Missing compound rule:** If the Overview mentions a compound not in the database table, flag it for verification. Add a USDA/quantitative or curated supplementary row when defensible; then admit it to Substances. Unsupported mentions must be removed or qualified; they must not generate cards or canonical pages. See `system/food-page-model.md` and `system/food-nutrition-schema.md`.

## Overview specification

Follow the **Overview editorial standard** in `system/food-page-model.md`. Almonds is the calibration example. Recommended depth (`short` / `standard` / `extended` in `system/food-page-letter-audit-schema.md`) still governs how much distinctive story a page may carry.

Mention macros or micronutrients when they materially characterise the food, using values consistent with the rendered table. Do not repeat their general biology.

Prioritise:

- characteristic substances and chemical forms;
- distinctive composition;
- direct matrix effects;
- preparation or processing;
- formulation variability;
- antinutrients and bioavailability;
- practical dietary or culinary role.

The Overview must:

- reflect the food itself (intrinsic compounds only as identity constituents)
- reflect well-established, food-specific research rather than generic nutrient lectures
- mention only intrinsic compounds as present in the food (no downstream metabolites)
- read like editorial prose, not stitched bullet notes
- stay honest when the food’s role is culinary-support

The Overview must not:

- dump the nutrient table or copy NutritionTable rows
- invent biological importance for an ordinary staple or recipe starch
- present supplement, substance-class, or neighbouring-food evidence as direct evidence for the food
- list long mechanistic chains or speculative pathways
- mention downstream microbial metabolites as if they are present in the food (e.g. SCFAs from fibre)

Detailed sourcing, pairing how-to, and recipe method belong in Food Context / Recipes. Overview may name a distinctive matrix or culinary role.

## Food-index descriptions

The YAML `description` field is the Foods Index card. Follow **Food-index descriptions** in `system/food-page-model.md`. Do not add a second index field.

## Key Nutritional Highlights specification

Add `## Key Nutritional Highlights` immediately after `## Overview`. Follow the Highlights layer in `system/food-page-model.md`. Almonds is the calibration example. Highlights are takeaways, not an evidence table.

- Use 3-6 bullet points only.
- Keep each bullet to one sentence or short clause.
- Include both notable benefits and relevant constraints where useful.
- Use neutral, scientific wording (no "strengths/weaknesses" or "pros/cons" labels).
- Avoid vague filler language; prefer food-specific, decision-relevant points.
- Do not inherit trial-log detail removed from Overviews.
- Repetition is allowed only when function differs:
  - Highlights = brief summary
  - later sections = expanded explanation/context/mechanism/preparation
- Do not list all amino acids in Highlights. If protein quality is relevant, summarize briefly (e.g. "complete protein with high digestibility" or "lysine-limited; complements legumes").

### Specificity Rule

Only include points that are distinctive to the specific food.

Do NOT include:
- generic statements that apply to most foods in the same category,
- comparisons to obviously inferior baselines (e.g. "better than refined grains"),
- general macronutrient statements that do not differentiate the food (e.g. "provides carbohydrates"),
- properties common to essentially all foods in the category (e.g. "low in saturated fat" for most plant foods),
- recipe method dumps (detailed preparation belongs in Food Context). Culinary-support pages may note culinary role.
- trial doses, durations, detailed comparators, or study methods (belong in the reference annotation)

Each bullet should be answerable as:
- "What is uniquely or meaningfully characteristic about this food in the BRAIN Diet context?"

Culinary-support foods may answer that with culinary role. Do not invent a neurological narrative.

### Processing vs Food Identity Rule

Do not conflate intrinsic food properties with processing effects.

- Intrinsic properties (nutrients, amino acids, micronutrients) belong in:
  - Overview
  - Key Nutritional Highlights
- Processing-related effects belong in:
  - Food Context
  - Preparation

Highlights may include a neutral line like:
- "Nutritional profile depends on processing method."

Avoid statements that imply the whole food is harmful based only on specific processed forms.

## Amino acid / EAA logic

- **Animal foods** (fish, meat, eggs, dairy): Do not list all amino acids. Use a short line such as "**Protein profile:** Complete essential amino acid profile." Use front matter `protein_profile_note` if you want it data-driven. Only highlight individual amino acids when mechanistically relevant (e.g. turkey → tryptophan).
- **Plant foods** (legumes, grains, nuts, seeds): Do not dump every amino acid. Do highlight strengths, limiting amino acids, and complementary pairings. Use front matter: `amino_acid_strengths`, `limiting_amino_acids`, `complementary_pairings`. Example: "Lysine-rich relative to grains; lower in methionine. Pair with rice or oats for complete profile."

## Page structure (canonical order)

Section order reflects BRAIN framework: context before data.

1. **Overview** – Follow the Overview specification above. Short culinary-support pages may be one paragraph; standard/extended pages typically two. Inline numeric citations [1], [2]. Optionally add one line noting **protein profile** where relevant.
2. **Key Nutritional Highlights** – 3-6 concise bullets summarizing the most decision-relevant properties with neutral wording (benefits and constraints), following the rule above.
3. **Food Context** – Framework and practical context immediately after Highlights.
   - Use at most three subsections: **Sourcing**, **Synergies**, **Preparation**.
   - **Only include a subsection if it contains meaningful, evidence-backed content for that food.** Do not show placeholder text, generic filler, or empty/null sections.
   - Before omitting a subsection, first check whether there is a credible food-specific point that can be made from:
     - established nutritional science
     - preparation science
     - food matrix / bioavailability literature
     - practical dietary context
   - **Sourcing**: show only if there is a meaningful distinction such as grass-fed vs grain-fed, wild vs farmed, organic vs conventional, minimally processed vs processed, or contamination/quality differences. If no such distinction is relevant or evidence-backed, omit Sourcing entirely.
   - **Preparation**: show only if handling or cooking method meaningfully changes nutrient retention, bioavailability, oxidation/AGE formation, microbial safety, food structure/digestibility, or practical use in the diet. Keep this section focused on process, not meal composition. If nothing meaningful can be said, omit Preparation entirely.
   - **Synergies**: often the most relevant subsection, because food interactions shape bioavailability or physiological effect (e.g. vitamin C with non‑heme iron, fat with carotenoids, grains with legumes for amino acid complementarity, herbs/spices reducing oxidative products in cooked foods). However, **omit Synergies** if no meaningful, food‑relevant interaction can be identified. Do not invent generic or weak pairings just to fill the section.
   - General rule: a shorter Food Context with only one or two strong subsections is better than three weak ones. **Quality over completeness**—every visible subsection should add genuine scientific or practical value.
4. **Essential Amino Acid Profile** – When required (protein ≥5 g/100 g or commonly protein source). Keep brief and functional; avoid full amino-acid dumps.
5. **Nutrition (per 100 g)** – `<NutritionTable details={frontMatter} />`. Renders **sub-tables**: Core nutrients, Key vitamins and minerals, Bioactive compounds (publicly admitted individual fatty acids from `nutrition_per_100g` + `nutrition_supplementary_sources`), and optional **Functional metrics** (`nutrition_functional_metrics`). Source notes for supplementary (`*`) rows appear below the block. Component renders when `nutrition_per_100g` has entries, or supplementary sources, or functional metrics are present.
6. **Substances** – `<FoodSubstancesFromTable details={frontMatter} />` when using the nutrition layer; otherwise `<FoodSubstances details={frontMatter} />`
7. **Recipes** – `<FoodRecipes tag="food tag" />`
8. **References** – Local, page-level reference list for that food, containing only real citations that support claims on the page. Use Vancouver-style numeric citations [1], [2] in Overview/Food Context/Highlights as needed, and list the full clickable references here (ideally also present in `docs/papers/BRAIN-Diet-References`).

## References and connection to BibTeX

- Each reference link targets the global bibliography with a fragment: `/docs/papers/BRAIN-Diet-References#citationKey` (e.g. `#yang_metal_oysters_2024`). The **citation key** must match the key of an entry in `static/bibtex/BRAIN-diet.bib`.
- **Bibliographic core:** `[n] Author(s) (Year). [{Paper title}](/docs/papers/BRAIN-Diet-References#citationKey)`.
- **Food-page extension:** References retain the project-wide Author (Year) and linked-title core. Food pages may append a concise food-specific finding or trial highlight because study detail is normally omitted from the Overview.
  `[n] Author(s) (Year). [{Paper title}](/docs/papers/BRAIN-Diet-References#citationKey). Food-relevant finding.`
- **Fail condition:** A reference line without a bibliography link is invalid. Bullet prefixes, stub author-only links, neighbouring-abstract text, or leading dietary advice in the annotation are invalid.
- Resolve each annotation **only** from the exact citation key and bounded BibTeX entry. Never borrow a neighbouring abstract.
- Do not retain a reference merely because it was previously present.
- Citation correctness and citation relevance are separate. Do not treat a correctly joined but generic/mismatched paper as a join error (`system/food-page-letter-audit-schema.md`).
- Minimum **two relevant** references. Prefer direct food/formulation analysis, human evidence involving the food, characteristic-substance or matrix evidence confirmed in that food, food-specific preparation evidence, or a high-quality food-specific review. Table quantities for ordinary fibre, protein, fat, carbohydrate, vitamins, and minerals do not need papers. Generic mechanism papers cannot meet the floor unless labelled as context and genuinely needed. Never present supplement, substance-class, or neighbouring-food evidence as direct evidence for the food. Flag weak evidence rather than padding.
- If a claim has no bibliography anchor yet, add the BibTeX entry first, ensure it renders on `/docs/papers/BRAIN-Diet-References`, then link that anchor from the reference line.
- **Link only works if the entry appears on the page.** The BRAIN-Diet-References page is built from the same .bib file but **deduplicates** entries by DOI, URL, or title+year+author. If two entries share the same DOI or URL, only one is shown (the one with richer metadata). The visible entry’s anchor is its own citation key. So:
  - Having the key in the .bib file is **necessary** but **not sufficient**: the entry must be the one that survives deduplication, or no other entry may have the same DOI/URL.
  - When adding a **new** BibTeX entry and a food-page link to it: avoid reusing the same DOI or URL as an existing entry; otherwise the new entry may be omitted and the fragment link will not scroll to it.
- **After adding a new reference:** Verify that the reference appears on the [BRAIN-Diet-References](/docs/papers/BRAIN-Diet-References) page and that the link from the food page (e.g. `.../BRAIN-Diet-References#kacser_control_1973`) scrolls to that entry. If the entry is missing on the page, fix deduplication (remove duplicate DOI/URL elsewhere or ensure your entry has the metadata that wins) or use the citation key that actually appears.

See `system/food-page-schema.md`, `system/food-page-model.md`, `system/food-page-letter-audit-schema.md`, and `system/food-nutrition-schema.md` for full schema. Use `docs/foods/dark-chocolate.md` for overall page structure; A-food pages (Almonds) for the food-reference annotation extension; `salmon.md` and `lentils.md` for front matter shapes.

## Letter-audit guardrails

- Preserve Overview → rendered table → editorial Substance admission. Overview register follows `system/food-page-model.md`.
- Ordinary or trace presence does not automatically justify a card.
- Do not create Substance pages during the audit.
- Do not remove mechanisms until their destination is recorded.
- Keep simple foods simple. Recommended depth is short / standard / extended.
- Do not replace the Author (Year) and linked-title bibliographic core. Food pages may append a finding. Do not begin the next letter batch from a schema-only change.
